# 4.1 Governed tools as UC functions

The CSV snapshot was a copy. Unity Catalog functions read the source tables directly, carry their own descriptions for the model, and are granted like any other object.

1. Open `step4-agent-bricks/uc_functions.sql` from the lab files.
2. Replace every `<catalog>` with your catalog and every `<schema>` with `{{ n.schema }}`.
3. Open the **SQL editor**, select your SQL warehouse, paste the file and click **Run all**.
4. Verify that four functions now exist in `<catalog>.{{ n.schema }}`: `get_booking`, `list_guest_bookings`, `quote_refund` and `get_refund_policy`.
5. Test the refund logic against the three bookings:

    ```sql
    SELECT * FROM <catalog>.{{ n.schema }}.quote_refund(532)
    UNION ALL SELECT * FROM <catalog>.{{ n.schema }}.quote_refund(166)
    UNION ALL SELECT * FROM <catalog>.{{ n.schema }}.quote_refund(703);
    ```

6. Verify the results:

| booking_id | total_paid | days_before_check_in | refund_amount |
|---|---|---|---|
{% for c in f.cases %}
| {{ c.booking }} | {{ c.paid }} | {{ c.days }} | {{ c.refund }} |
{% endfor %}

These are the same numbers the CSV version produced, now computed from governed tables. Open a function in **Catalog Explorer** and click **Lineage** to see it reads from `samples.wanderbricks`.

!!! tip "Who can run the tools"
    A user needs `EXECUTE` on the function plus `SELECT` on the tables it reads. To keep a support agent away from guest emails, simply do not return them, as these functions do. For column masks and row filters, see the Unity Catalog documentation.

